# ~/.chrome-clients/<client>.bypass
#
# Extra proxy-bypass entries for `work browse <client>`. Hosts listed here are
# routed DIRECT instead of through the ssh -D SOCKS tunnel.
#
# Why this exists: `ssh -D` forwards TCP only. Video calls (WebRTC) carry their
# audio/video over UDP, which cannot traverse the tunnel. If a call's media
# servers go through the proxy, the call freezes/drops. Bypassing the
# conferencing hosts lets their media flow over the Mac's real interface so the
# call works. The base list already bypasses Google (Meet/GApps/YouTube).
#
# Format: one entry per line. `#` comments and blank lines are ignored. Entries
# may be hostnames, wildcards (*.example.com), IP literals, or CIDR ranges.
# Chrome's --proxy-bypass-list syntax applies.
#
# This file is Mac-local and MUST NOT be committed (see docs/client-boundaries.md).
# Copy it to ~/.chrome-clients/<client>.bypass and fill in real hosts there.

# --- Example: Microsoft Teams (incl. Class.com and similar Teams wrappers) ---
# Signaling / web app:
# *.teams.microsoft.com
# *.teams.microsoft.us
# *.skype.com
# *.lync.com
# *.sfbassets.com
# *.office.com
# *.office365.com
# *.microsoftonline.com
# *.azureedge.net
# *.trafficmanager.net
#
# Teams real-time media (UDP) — Microsoft's published "Optimize" IP ranges:
# 13.107.64.0/18
# 52.112.0.0/14
# 52.122.0.0/15
#
# Conferencing wrapper (replace with the real host you connect to):
# *.<conferencing-host>
